WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.265
Performance issues in local inference often fall into two categories:

00:00:04.265 --> 00:00:06.680
slow prompt processing and slow generation.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:10.585
Prompt processing is the work of reading and embedding the input context.

00:00:10.585 --> 00:00:12.469
Generation is the token-by-token output step.

00:00:12.469 --> 00:00:14.670
A setup may load quickly but generate slowly,

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:17.720
or handle short prompts well but struggle with long context.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.850
Knowing which phase is slow helps narrow the cause.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:22.320
Memory pressure is one of the most common problems.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.809
A model, quantization, or context length may exceed available VRAM or system RAM.

00:00:26.809 --> 00:00:30.770
GPU offload settings may be too aggressive, causing failures or instability.

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:33.340
CPU-only execution may work but feel slow.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.920
Disk speed can affect model loading time, especially with large files.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:38.520
Batch size and thread settings can help,

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.805
but they should be adjusted carefully and tested with the actual workload.

00:00:41.805 --> 00:00:45.945
Sampling settings affect output behavior and can be mistaken for performance or model

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:46.850
quality issues.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:49.240
Temperature, top-p, repetition controls,

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:53.280
and related options influence how deterministic or varied the output feels.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.150
If results seem rambling, repetitive, or inconsistent,

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:59.655
the issue may be sampling configuration, prompt formatting, model fit,

00:00:59.655 --> 00:01:00.700
or quantization quality.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.850
Troubleshooting should separate runtime settings from model capability.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:07.355
A practical troubleshooting loop starts with a known compatible runtime,

00:01:07.355 --> 00:01:11.090
a trusted model file, conservative context length, and a documented baseline.

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:15.230
Then change one variable at a time: quantization, GPU offload, thread count,

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:17.439
context length, or sampling settings.

00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:19.840
Compare memory use, speed, and output quality.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.135
This disciplined approach is slower than random tweaking,

00:01:22.135 --> 00:01:24.020
but it produces knowledge you can reuse.
